+# Scoring and prizes
+
+## Data and invariants
+
+- `Quiz.number_of_winners` defaults to 3 and must be an integer >= 1. Both the form and model enforce this; SQLite also has a positive-value check. `Game.for_quiz!` snapshots the value alongside the question content. Changing settings only affects future games.
+- Each transition to `open` records one `GameQuestion.opened_at` timestamp in the same locked transaction as the game revision. No phone starts its own timer. Closing or stale/repeated transitions cannot alter the opening time.
+- `Game.answer!` sets `Answer.picked_at` using the server clock on **every accepted submission**, even if its option index is unchanged. Client timestamps/IDs are not trusted. The last accepted pick counts; late answers after Finish cannot change either the selection or its timestamp.
+- One correct final answer = one point, including questions with several marked-correct options. Incorrect and missing answers contribute neither points nor time. Elapsed time uses microsecond-precision timestamps and is summed only across correct answers.
+- `Game#leaderboard` is shared by the stage, phone standings, and prize selection. It sorts by points descending, elapsed microseconds ascending, then player ID (join order) for exactly equal scores and times. Places are ordinal: 1, 2, 3, …, with every registered player included, even if disconnected. No cumulative points are rendered on phones while the game is running.
+- `Player.winner` is persisted per game. `select_winners!` only runs on ended games, under a game lock, and atomically replaces the winner set and increments the revision. No answer or ranking changes are caused by drawing prizes.
+- **Top N** uses the ordered positive-score entries. **Draw** guarantees players with points greater than the N-th place and samples uniformly without replacement from the full boundary-points group; speed is irrelevant to that sample. Fewer than N positive-score players means all positive-score players win. A fresh sample is taken on every click; it may legitimately match the previous draw. Moderator clicks are queued in order rather than collapsed into one command. Reset clears everyone.
+- HTTP controls require moderator Basic auth and Rails CSRF protection. Private player results are derived from the encrypted identity cookie. There are no public prize-writing endpoints.
+
+## Live behavior
+
+The existing in-process Action Cable transport broadcasts tiny invalidations after commits. Clients fetch their role-appropriate rendered state immediately, coalescing notifications within one event-loop turn only. Removing the old 80–250 ms randomized delay matters now that speed determines rank. All phones receive the same question opening timestamp; network transit/processing latency is unavoidable, but there is no deliberate per-player delay or polling-only game progression.
+
+Ended screens **remain subscribed**, and loading an already ended game also subscribes. Prize changes use the same monotonic revision as phase changes, so stale HTTP responses cannot overwrite newer winners. A reconnected or foregrounded screen reads current state; a one-second fallback while disconnected and a 20-second connected safety check recover missed events. Presence lease renewal stops at the end, but presence does not influence final places or prize eligibility.
+
+Production remains one foreground Puma process using local SQLite and Action Cable's async adapter. No worker, Redis, CDN, or external service is needed. Dependencies are unchanged and installed.
+
+## Existing data and portability
+
+The migration supplies three prizes for existing quizzes/games and clears winner flags by default. Runs recorded before this feature have no reliable question-open/last-pick timing: their correct answers still score, but missing timing contributes zero elapsed time. We do not invent client timings for historical data. Start a new game to obtain fully timed results.
+
+Quiz JSON now includes `number_of_winners`; omitted values default to three so old documents remain importable. Explicit values must be JSON integers >= 1. Players, ranks, answer timings, and winner flags are never exported or imported.
+
+## Verification
+
+- `bin/ci`: installed dependencies, migrations, Ruby style, security, eager loading, and all Rails tests.
+- `test/models/prizes_test.rb`: settings/snapshots, final-pick timing, microsecond totals, deterministic exact ties, multiple-correct questions, point-only redraws (seeded random generators), reset/broadcasts, N=1, fewer scoring players than prizes, empty/all-zero games, and invalid commands.
+- `test/integration/prizes_flow_test.rb`: field labels and validation, hidden in-game points, final list rows and phone places, live version changes, persisted marks, permissions, CSRF, and legacy/new JSON.
+- `script/browser_prizes_smoke.rb`: five independent Chromium phones, moderator and second big-screen browser, real production WebSockets, latency measurements, last-pick ranking, Top N, repeated Draw, Reset winners, phone announcements, ended-screen reload subscriptions, and no navigation during updates. Run with `bin/serve` on port 3000 and moderator/test-password, or override APP_URL and the moderator credentials. Screenshots go under `storage/screenshots/`.
+- `bin/rails runner script/live_concurrency_smoke.rb`: separate SQLite connections test answer/Finish races and concurrent winner-selection transactions.
+
+Local verification saw the first question arrive on six observing screens 93–120 ms after Start; this is an observed local measurement, not a network latency guarantee.
